			<div class="content" id="post-content-1927577">
									

<p>As always, this is all opinion. I'm not a veteran member or staff, so keep that in mind.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>What to do with B and W in public? What about the captured ones? What to do, when there is a disproportional number of either? What to do with the area during the event? And then: How? etc., etc.</p>
</blockquote>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Long&nbsp;Response</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Hide&nbsp;whatever</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>In a REAL scientific document&#8230; you would be right. If the Foundation were real, each SCP would be dozens of pages long. Hell, each Containment Procedure would be at LEAST dozens of pages long, detailing each and every possible permutation, every reaction for any possible action. I know this, because I do technical writing as my job, and I work for a huge government agency.</p>
<p>But the Foundation isn't real, and thus, you don't _actually_ need to prepare for every contingency. You only need to prepare for what you think your readers may mentally call you out on. In my experience here, you basically just need to show that <em>you</em> were thorough and your Foundation is putting enough thought into containing everything.</p>
<p>Specifically:<br />
Your first two sentences BOTH talk about containing the battlefields. You probably only need the first sentence, as the second presents a ton of useless information (is the size of the battlefields important?) and has an SCP acronym which might be a turn-off for some. Next sentence is fine. Then, you don't really need the number of MTF stations built, that's really just implied when you mention MTF at all. Next sentence is fine. But then, do you really need to go into how the Foundation gets its temperature data? Do you need to mention that they have their own temperature satellites? And then you go into a contingency plan about what happens if the satellite is <em>down.</em> Unless this skip has something to do with that satellite and interfering with it, I think you can just get rid of that whole piece and I guarantee you no one will question you. The Foundation has so many assets, you may assume they can get reliable temperature data without anyone questioning you.</p>
<p>The rest is okay, but you might get rid of the tracking from birth portion. It opens too many questions. How do they know for sure who's an instance? Etc?</p>
<p>Just my two cents.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

